# Git hooks must keep LF endings.
#
# This machine has core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites checked-out files to CRLF.
# For a /bin/sh script that is fatal rather than cosmetic: the shebang becomes
# "#!/bin/sh\r" and the shell reports
#     bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M: no such file or directory
# so the hook silently stops running and the changelog quietly stops updating.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the Gradle wrapper script, which is also run by sh.
android-twa/gradlew text eol=lf

# CHANGELOG.md is generated, and the generator writes LF. Without this, autocrlf
# rewrites the working copy to CRLF on every checkout while the committed blob stays
# LF, so `npm run changelog -- --check` compares LF-generated text against a CRLF
# file and reports the whole file as stale — a CI guard that always fails. Git sees
# no change either way (it normalizes on commit), so this only affects the on-disk
# copy the generator actually compares against.
CHANGELOG.md text eol=lf

# The wrapper jar and the signed Android artifacts are binary — never touch them.
*.jar binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.keystore binary
